jules: FYI onboarding ran into a subtle ordering bug in the Node embed batching helper. When some chunked requests partially fail and we retry items individually, the helper appends results as they arrive, which can scramble the original input order and break rerank consumers that match by index.

meera: Can you paste the simplified helper? Node or py?

jules: Node. Simplified version:

```js
// chunk concurrently, push arrivals into an array
const results = [];
await Promise.all(chunks.map(async c => {
  try {
    const r = await client.embeddings.create({input: c.map(i=>i.text)});
    results.push(...r.data);
  } catch (e) {
    for (const it of c) {
      const single = await client.embeddings.create({input:[it.text]});
      results.push(single.data[0]);
    }
  }
}));
return results; // vulnerable to arrival-order races
```

cory: Yep that's the issue. Helpers must guarantee input-order. Quick fix: allocate output array of same length and write embeddings back to their original indices. If server doesn't echo ids, keep a local map before sending.

meera: Also add tests that randomly delay responses to simulate the race. And document that SDK helpers guarantee positional reassembly so app code doesn't assume server will echo ids.

jules: I'll open a PR to: add index-preserving merge, expose an option to request server echo (echo_ids), and add unit+integration tests simulating chunk races. Will add a note in sample-app README.

ops-bot: ci/test-embed-helper: unit + integration tests queued for @jules branch.

cory: Might be worth a parity sweep after this — Python helper already has index mapping in a previous change, Node lagged. Good catch.

jules: Thanks — patch incoming. :sparkles:
